Hello,

I've been having several issue with monitor processes achieving quorum 
with 0.60. The most recent was an assert with the output mon/Monitor.cc: 
1105: FAILED assert(0 == "We should never reach this"). I've pasted a 
larger set of the log file at the bottom of this email.

Prior to this assert, I ran into trouble where one out of the three 
monitors would drop out of quorum with the other two. Clocks are 
synchronized properly. Over the past week, I've seen several instances 
of a monitor dropping out of quorum, but the process continues to run in 
the probing state without ever achieving recovery.

I've seen several odd behaviors as I try to recover from this state:

1) Restarting that monitor that is out of quorum does not fix the issue.

2) If I leave the OSDs running, then do a rolling restart of the 
monitors, I can get the original monitor that was our of quorum to come 
back into quorum, but then one of the other monitors falls out or 
quorum. I've repeated this issue several times. In this state, I've 
always seen one leader, one peon, and one probing. The order in which I 
start the monitors seems to determine which will end up in the probing 
state. I have had each of the three end up probing during my investigation.

3) If I stop all mon and osd daemons, I can bring the three mons back up 
and they achieve quorum immediately. Then I can restart the OSDs. I've 
sometimes seen one mon fall back out of quorum soon afterward.

4) I've seen a situation where mon.a and mon.b were concurrently 
reporting as the leader while mon.c was a peon. Ceph -s would report one 
of the leaders and the peon as part of the quorum.

Interestingly, after the assert shown in the log below, I restarted that 
monitor, quorum was immediately achieved between all three, and it has 
been stable for the past 18 hours.

Any ideas?

Thanks,
Mike


